Monitoring and alerting setup for a production microservices architecture:

1. **Kubernetes Monitoring**: Use a Kubernetes-based monitoring system like Prometheus, Grafana, and Alertmanager to collect metrics from your microservices. This will provide insights into CPU usage, memory, and other resource utilization.

   Recommended setup:
   - Prometheus: Collect metrics from your microservices and store them in a time-series database like InfluxDB or OpenTSDB.
   - Grafana: Visualize the collected metrics using Grafana, which can be accessed through a web interface.
   - Alertmanager: Set up Alertmanager to trigger alerts based on the collected metrics. You can configure Alertmanager to send emails, Slack messages, or other notification channels.

2. **Service Discovery**: Implement a service discovery mechanism like etcd, Consul, or ZooKeeper to manage the registration and discovery of microservices. This will help you identify which microservice is running and which endpoint it provides.

3. **API Gateway**: Use an API Gateway like NGINX, AWS API Gateway, or Google Cloud Endpoints to manage API requests and routes. This will help you route requests to the correct microservice and handle authentication and authorization.

4. **Logging**: Set up logging for your microservices using a logging framework like ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana), Splunk, or Loggly. This will help you monitor the health and performance of your microservices.

5. **Alerting**: Use an alerting tool like PagerDuty, Splunk, or Datadog to set up alerts based on the collected metrics and API Gateway logs. This will help you quickly identify issues and respond to them.

6. **Security**: Implement security measures like SSL/TLS encryption, authentication, and authorization to protect your microservices and API Gateway.

7. **Monitoring Tools**: Use monitoring tools like New Relic, Datadog, or Sumo Logic to monitor the performance and latency of your microservices.

8. **CI/CD**: Implement a Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline to automate the build, test, and deployment of your microservices. This will help you catch errors and issues early in the development process.

Example Terraform configuration for setting up a Kubernetes cluster with Prometheus, Grafana, Alertmanager, and NGINX:
```
resource "kubernetes_namespace" "example" {
  name = "example-namespace"
}

resource "kubernetes_service" "example" {
  name     = "example-service"
  namespace = kubernetes_namespace.example.name
  type     = "ClusterIP"
  port     = 80
  selector = {
    app = "example-app"
  }
}

resource "kubernetes_deployment" "example" {
  name     = "example-deployment"
  namespace = kubernetes_namespace.example.name
  replicas = 3
  selector {
    match_labels = {
      app = "example-app"
    }
  }
  template {
    metadata {
      labels = {
        app = "example-app"
      }
    }
    spec {
      containers {
        name = "example-container"
        image = "example-image"
        ports {
          container_port = 80
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

resource "kubernetes_ingress" "example" {
  name     = "example-ingress"
  namespace = kubernetes_namespace.example.name
  class   = "nginx"
  rules   = [
    {
      host    = "example.com"
      http    = "https"
      path    = "/"
      backend = {
        service = kubernetes_service.example.name
      }
    }
  ]
}

resource "kubernetes_service_account" "example" {
  name     = "example-service-account"
  namespace = kubernetes_namespace.example.name
  role    = "ServiceAccount/Default"
}

resource "kubernetes_cluster_role" "example" {
  name = "example-cluster-role"
}

resource "kubernetes_cluster_role_binding" "example" {
  name   = "example-cluster-role-binding"
  role  = kubernetes_cluster_role.example.name
  service_account = kubernetes_service_account.example.name
}
```
This is just an example configuration, and you should adjust it to fit your specific use case.

